[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Did you know that LiDAR sensors on your iPhone have a secret superpower? Turns out they can see around corners. A team of researchers at the MIT Media Lab just published a paper proving that a cheap under $100 consumer LiDAR system can perform non-line-of-site imaging, a'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trick that used to require a $50,000 laboratory setup. The physics behind it is quite interesting. So, LiDAR works by firing laser pulses and measuring the picosecond time-of-flight. Normally, the sensor only registers direct'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'line-of-site reflections. But, when a laser pulse hits a wall, the light scatters. Some of those photons bounce off the wall, strike a hidden object around the corner, bounce back to the wall, and finally scatter back into the sensor. The catch is that this'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multi-bounce return signal is incredibly faint and noisy. A single frame from a consumer sensor only gives you a chaotic mess of data points. To solve this, the researchers developed an algorithm called motion-induced aperture sampling. So, instead of fighting the natural hand'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'jitter of a smartphone or the vibration of a robot, they turned it into an asset. It functions similarly to how burst photography stacks noisy frames to create a clean low-light photo, or how satellites use synthetic aperture radar'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to turn motion into spatial resolution. The algorithm models the object\'s shape, its motion, and the camera\'s exact position over time into [clears throat] a unified multi-frame measurement model. By enforcing temporal coherence across a'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sequence of frames, the system strips away the noise and extracts the underlying signal. And the results are pretty staggering for a sensor with roughly 100 pixels. The system can reconstruct 3D shapes of completely hidden static objects, track multiple'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moving targets out of view, and even use hidden landmarks to calculate the camera\'s own spatial location in real time. The researchers have already open-sourced the code, which opens up massive possibilities for robotics,'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'autonomous vehicles, and AR/VR tracking without relying on expensive proprietary hardware. If you want to stay up to date with the latest engineering insights, be sure to subscribe to the Better Stack channel.'
